Falsifies `elf::verification::a_passing_check_yields_an_in_bounds_range`.

The defect: the in-file test checks where the segment starts rather than where it ends,
`p_offset > file_len` in place of `end > file_len`. "The segment must be inside the file" is
true of both spellings as English, and a file whose segments are all small satisfies them
equally, which is every file this tree loads.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(end <= file_len)`, on a segment starting inside the
file and running past its end, where `segment_at` then slices `bytes[p_offset..end]` beyond the
buffer the kernel read.

diff --git a/crates/elf/src/parse.rs b/crates/elf/src/parse.rs
index 51de81b47..bed69d945 100644
--- a/crates/elf/src/parse.rs
+++ b/crates/elf/src/parse.rs
@@ -222,7 +222,7 @@ pub(crate) fn check_segment_bounds(
     let end = p_offset
         .checked_add(filesz)
         .ok_or(Error::SegmentOutOfBounds)?;
-    if end > file_len {
+    if p_offset > file_len {
         return Err(Error::SegmentOutOfBounds);
     }
 
